event: content_block_delta
data: {"delta":{"partial_json":"{\"file_path\": \"/tmp/mission-summary.md\", \"content\": \"# Mission m1: greet, tested — outcome: campaign-met\\n\\n## Result\\n\\ndev delivered and I independently verified all three required criteria on\\nbranch `cs-sandbox/<DEV:1>.<GROUP:1>`, commit `1461d3f`\\n(base `08603cc`):\\n\\n1. `src/greet.py` — `greet(name)` returns `f\\\"Hello, {name}!\\\"`, matching the\\n   required `\\\"Hello, \u003cname\u003e!\\\"` format.\\n2. `tests/test_greet.py` — unittest `TestCase` with `test_normal_name`\\n   (`greet(\\\"World\\\") == \\\"Hello, World!\\\"`) and `test_empty_string`\\n   (`greet(\\\"\\\") == \\\"Hello, !\\\"`), covering both required cases.\\n3. `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` exits 0 — verified myself in a\\n   clean `git worktree` checked out at dev's exact commit (not just taking\\n   dev's report on faith): both tests pass, exit code 0.\\n\\nScope was respected: only `src/greet.py` and `tests/test_greet.py` were\\ntouched.\\n\\n## Process\\n\\n- Dispatched full spec to dev (d002) in one round; no rework was needed.\\n- Verified by fetching dev's branch and running the test suite myself in an\\n  isolated worktree, rather than trusting the reported output.\\n- Accepted dev's work (d002).\\n\\n## Orchestrator branch\\n\\nI wrote no code myself, per my brief — my own branch remains at base, as\\nexpected for the orchestrator role in this mission.\\n\"}","type":"input_json_delta"},"index":1,"type":"content_block_delta"}

